{"step_index":515,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:44:15Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-08-23T16:43:51Z sender=e9e5fe1b-5bcf-4993-95b1-89d16fcd0020 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=Here is the summary and hardware requirement assessment for each of the 6 repositories:\n\n### 1. **HyperFrames**\n* **Summary:** HyperFrames is an open-source video rendering framework that turns plain HTML, CSS, media, and seekable web animations (GSAP, Lottie, Three.js) into deterministic MP4 videos using headless Chrome (Puppeteer) and FFmpeg.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** It runs lightweight on CPU via Node.js (≥22) and FFmpeg without needing a dedicated GPU or high RAM.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Project AIRI**\n* **Summary:** Project AIRI is an open-source AI VTuber and digital companion platform (inspired by Neuro-sama) integrating LLMs, Live2D/VRM avatars, speech recognition/synthesis, and autonomous gameplay capabilities across web, desktop, and mobile.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** It operates directly in web browsers or Electron via WebGPU/WASM and offloads heavy AI inference (LLM, TTS, ASR) to cloud APIs by default, though optional local GPU acceleration (via CUDA/Metal/Ollama) is supported if desired.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **MoneyPrinterTurbo**\n* **Summary:** MoneyPrinterTurbo is an all-in-one AI short-video creation tool that takes a topic or keyword to automatically generate scripts, source matching stock/local video footage, synthesize speech and subtitles, and produce HD short-form videos.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** The default setup requires only 4 GB RAM and a standard CPU (using cloud LLMs and Edge TTS); a dedicated GPU (4GB–8GB+ VRAM) is purely optional for local `faster-whisper` transcription or intensive batch rendering.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **OpenMontage**\n* **Summary:** OpenMontage is an open-source, agentic video production system that enables AI coding assistants to conduct live research, write scripts, generate assets, and assemble finished videos using Remotion, HyperFrames, and FFmpeg.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** The standard pipeline runs smoothly on CPU using Node.js, FFmpeg, Piper TTS, and cloud generation APIs or stock media; a dedicated GPU is only needed if choosing to run local video models (such as Wan 2.1 or Hunyuan).\n\n---\n\n### 5. **OpenReels**\n* **Summary:** OpenReels is a TypeScript-based AI production pipeline and Web UI that turns any prompt or topic into a publish-ready vertical short video with AI-generated research, script, voiceover, visuals, soundtrack, and Remotion rendering in a single command.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** All heavy generation tasks (LLM, images, video, music, TTS) are routed through cloud APIs (Google Gemini, OpenAI, Anthropic, ElevenLabs, fal.ai), while the local app runs on standard CPU memory via Node.js/Docker and Remotion.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **MemPalace**\n* **Summary:** MemPalace is a local-first, zero-cloud AI memory system that stores conversation histories and project knowledge verbatim in structured \"wings\" and \"drawers\" for high-accuracy semantic retrieval without summarization.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** It runs completely on CPU and minimal memory using ChromaDB/SQLite and lightweight local embedding models (~30 MB to ~300 MB disk space).\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
{"step_index":516,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:44:15Z","content":"I had a few AI agents scan through all those GitHub repositories for you to see exactly how they work, why people use them, and which ones will melt your laptop!\n\nHere is the breakdown of your `find-skill-repo\\all github repos` folder:\n\n### ❌ Delete These (Your laptop cannot handle them)\nThese repos require a heavy, dedicated NVIDIA Graphics Card and lots of RAM. Since you have an Intel i5 with 8GB RAM and integrated graphics, trying to run these will crash or freeze your laptop:\n*   **`unsloth` (62 MB)**: Used by developers to train and fine-tune large AI language models locally.\n*   **`comfyui` (43 MB)**: A complex node-based software used to generate AI images (like Stable Diffusion) locally.\n*   **`ruflo` (1.76 GB)**: A heavy framework for running multiple AI agents. Because it is taking up almost 2GB on your disk, it has likely downloaded heavy local models.\n*   **`speech-to-speech`, `OmniVoice`, & `ViMax`**: Tools used for real-time AI voice cloning and video manipulation. Real-time AI requires a dedicated GPU.\n\n### ✅ Keep These (Your laptop CAN handle them)\nThese repositories are perfectly safe for your laptop because they are lightweight code, or they offload all the heavy AI processing to the cloud (using APIs) instead of your hardware:\n\n**The Video Generators (These 3 do the exact same thing):**\n*   **`MoneyPrinterTurbo` (200 MB), `OpenReels` (64 MB), & `OpenMontage` (83 MB)**: Why do people use them? They are \"faceless channel\" automators. You type a topic, and they automatically write a script, generate AI voiceovers, pull stock footage, and edit a YouTube Short for you. They all serve the exact same purpose, but they work on your laptop because they send the heavy AI work to cloud APIs!\n\n**The Code Tools:**\n*   **`HyperFrames` (569 MB)**: A lightweight coding framework that turns HTML, CSS, and web animations into MP4 videos using just your CPU.\n*   **`airi` (558 MB)**: An open-source AI VTuber platform (like Neuro-sama) for creating an animated digital streamer.\n*   **`mempalace` (60 MB)**: A lightweight local memory system for AI agents to remember old conversations.\n\n### 🗑️ Your Remotion Question\nYou are **100% correct** about Remotion! You do **not** need the `remotion-main` (712 MB) folder. As you read, typing `npx create-video@latest` automatically downloads exactly what you need on the fly for your specific project. You can safely delete the entire `remotion-main` folder right now to save space!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Specificity**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool selection, always opting for the most specific command available. Specifically, I am now committed to using `grep_search` instead of `cat` in bash commands to create or append to files. This guarantees that I am never inadvertently using an indirect method when a more precise tool is readily available.\n\n\n**Categorizing Repositories**\n\nMy focus has shifted to categorizing the GitHub repositories, particularly identifying those that pose a performance risk on the user's laptop, like `unsloth` and `comfyui`, requiring heavy resources and being unsuitable for the user's hardware. I'm also working on summarizing their functionality and use cases for the user, and clustering those with similar functionalities. I will also address the special case of the remotion folder.\n\n\n**Assessing Resource Demands**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on resource demands, specifically categorizing repositories by the compute power they require. I've flagged `ruflo` as heavy and potentially problematic due to its size and local model likely needing heavy resources. I have identified `speech-to-speech`, `OmniVoice`, and `ViMax`, as real-time AI tools that are GPU bound. My focus has shifted to also cataloging those that can use Cloud APIs instead. I am making distinctions between the three `Automated Video Makers` and